{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p9-34327fa9","text":"Server requires a real MySQL/MariaDB running at DB_HOST:DB_PORT. The .env is the user's — its DB probably points at their local MySQL, and the earlier attempt got "Access denied root" — so their MySQL may be listening but .env DB_USER/DB_PASSWORD are blocked/unknown. I cannot read .env. I could try MYSQL with defaults from the .env (server would use them automatically) — the failure was using password NO (default). So .env's DB_PASSWORD is likely set (external DB). Hmm, error was from EnsureDatabase with root/empty (EffectiveRootUser when DB_ROOT_USER unset → DBUser root... Access denied root@localhost using password NO means DB_PASSWORD wasn't set? Or .env has password and proton?? Actually EnsureDatabase uses EffectiveRootUser/EffectiveRootPassword — .env's DB_PASSWORD presumably is set; error "using password: NO" suggests the env password was EMPTY, meaning .env exists but maybe lacks DB_PASSWORD or godotenv didn't load because CWD... we ran from project root, so .env exists; DB_PASSWORD likely empty in it and root has no password → but error says access denied, i.e., MySQL requires a password.

Hmm. Actually MySQL may not even be running: "EsServidorMySQLArriba" check passed (otherwise different fatal message). It got "Access denied 1045" — so MySQL IS running (their production local?). Their DB password is in .env but godotenv... Hold on: my shell env had no DB_PASSWORD; godotenv loads .env into env: values stay. Let me check: EnsureDatabase got password from cfg.EffectiveRootPassword() = DB_ROOT_PASSWORD or DB_PASSWORD. If .env has DB_PASSWORD set for its app user, root password may differ. Their DB_USER may be custom user with privileges; root may not allow.

Options: try connecting with the .env's DBUser/DBPassword — that's what OpenMySQL uses. The fatal was in EnsureDatabase. If I skip EnsureDatabase? Can't without code change.

Better: run a throwaway MySQL? Heavy. Alternative: the test infrastructure uses sqlite In-Memory (interfaces/web tests pass using testdb). The web server requires MySQL though.

Maybe their real MySQL on this machine has credentials in .env but only usable as DB_USER, and root provisioning fails. In .env maybe DB_ROOT_USER/DB_ROOT_PASSWORD are set — but error said using password NO → both empty. So .env probably has DB_USER=erpgo or similar non-root as DBUser/DBPassword? If DBUser were set, EnsureDatabase uses EffectiveRootUser = DBUser... using password NO suggests DBPassword empty too?? Confusing: "Access denied for user 'root'@'localhost' (using password: NO)" — user root, no password. So cfg.EffectiveRootUser() = root → DB_ROOT_USER empty AND DB_USER=root; password empty.

So their local MySQL requires a password for root which I don't have. I can't read .env... but the .env file itself is blocked to me (contains secrets). The server reads it at startup. If DB_PASSWORD were set in .env, godotenv sets DB_PASSWORD env → then EnsureDatabase using password: NO wouldn't occur ("using password: NO" happens when empty). Unless os.LookupEnv sees DB_PASSWORD set with non-empty value, then password would be used → "using password: YES". So .env likely has empty DB_PASSWORD and their MySQL root has a password set → the user's local production .env doesn't match this machine's MySQL.

